A||||22||theogre||Product Review, Headlights, Halogen||||6||
Z||000000||theogre||11-12-1999||01:05 AM||||could also be titled Lakes of Light or I gotta wear shades.......<P>One of the most common night driving complaints is cruddy headlight output. And while you would think that Fiero's headlamp hieght is low, it's actually not as low as you think compaired to other cars. It's actually very close to the same as many cars thanks to the popup lights.<P>One of the first things I do when I get a car is change the headlights. Always to a good brand of Halogen. But even with a Halogen light there's allot to be desired. A very narrow dim low beam is the main problem.<P>Enter the aftermarket...... You've all seen the Hella and other lights, and the price. Those of you who've used them I'm sure got a better, if illegal in many cases, light for your money. Why illegal? there are a few reasons, but the main one is that DOT and many state regulations limit the brightness of "sealed beam" headlamps. Some readers can atest to being stopped because of using too bright lamps. Cops just don't like you if they think you've got the high beam on. Especially around "last call".......<P>Recently I ran into Philips "High Visibility" lamps at K-mart. At about 25$us for the pair, a bit less "on sale", I figured what the heck and tried a pair.<P>The short answer  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> ...... after driving around with them a bit, I'm planning to use them in every vehicle I drive from now on. When someone here saw I was trying them in another thread about easier headlamp changing, they told me I'd like them. They sure weren't wrong. ((I forgot who said it and I can't search it from this posting window, sorry.))<P>These lights are made in such a way that you are literally driving into a lake of light compaired to the tiny stream you get from standard Halogen lamps. They are completely DOT legal lamps. The Low beam flood area is huge yet it will not reach up above the lamp center line and blind anyone.<P>If you've been looking for better lamps, I strongly recomend you give these a try. At about 25$ for the pair you can't beat them. <P>Phillips part # H6054HV<BR>UPC code 46677-28391||||reg||6
Z||000001||lowCG||11-12-1999||02:47 AM||||I'm planning a road trip(2000 miles)here in a couple weeks,I'll get a set for the haul.<BR>Thanks for the tip.<BR>The expensive,motorcycle type headlights might not be as good a deal as they were fifteen years ago maybe?||||reg||1
Z||000002||Dan||11-13-1999||05:43 AM|||| Yeah Ogre that was me.I did like you said,I liked it so well I put them on all my vehicles and my kids cars too.Anyone trying these out should be very satisified.||||reg||1
Z||000003||WisconsinGT||11-13-1999||11:15 PM||||Good job guys! I just picked a pair from K-Mart. One thing though...If you are getting the Phillips lamps for an 88, you want Phillips part # H6054LL.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Gokart||11-14-1999||06:43 AM||||Never fails! I just changed mine to Sylvania "cool blue" (H6054CB), although I'm happier with them compared to the ones that were in there before. Very white!||||reg||1
Z||000005||Tim||11-14-1999||09:27 AM||||Please, what's the difference between H-6054-HV & H-6054-LL?  TIA||||reg||1
Z||000006||theogre||11-14-1999||01:02 PM||||All Fiero use a 6054 lamp body. That part of the number is standard to nearly all makes of lamp. The leading H, also pretty standard, designates Halogen lamps. The sufix if any is manufacturor specific. All 6054 body lamps are physically interchangable.<P>I think H6054LL is the standard lamp from philips. I'll look next time I go by the store. <P>the 6054HV High Visibility lamps, in a brown & black box labled "High Visibility Halogen", are physical replacemets that may not be listed in all books. May not even be listed in all the philips books currently in use. Often takes a while for the most recent books to get out.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 11-14-1999).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||Bill Osmer||11-14-1999||07:06 PM||||Ogre - as a good "citizen" of the Fiero community, I followed your lead as to changing over to the new Phillips H6054HVs - love them VERY high rating on the improvement scale.<BR>I analyzed the other choices - the H6054LLs are "Long Life" bulbs - no significant lighting improvement (some but not much). I never did find the "CBs" that GoKart referred to.<BR>Again, I'm not sorry I changed. <P>BTW - there are lots of sales going on - the prices are down to approx $20 or lower.||||reg||1
Z||000008||WisconsinGT||11-14-1999||11:51 PM||||Bill...are you saying that the LL bulbs are the same as the HV bulbs? Or are they a little better then the HV bulbs? Thanks||||reg||1
Z||000009||Bill Osmer||11-15-1999||08:44 AM||||I believe the HV bulbs are brighter. To be honest, it's a personal OPINION. I just took the time to read the specs on the packaging of the various suppliers' bulbs. In my experience, most of the time to get long life, a lower current (and usually lower brightness) is designed for the bulb. I have not performed a side-by-side comparison. I do not believe they are the same. I was looking for better brightness and better pattern, and these bulbs meet both criteria.||||reg||1
Z||000010||theogre||11-15-1999||10:48 AM||||all Philips lamps with LL in the number are just a bit tougher lamp than the basic ones. They are rated for more hours of burn time. They are otherwise identical to base lamps. The "long life" lamps also cost more, but are they worth it? I don't know. the regular ones last years.<P>HV lamps are better than the LL head lamps. The LL headlamp is like the rest of the line. It's just a base model lamp with longer burn time.<P>HV lamps have the whole lamp redesigned for better output from the same amount of light. <P>I don't know what the HV burn time rating is, but since headlamps usually last years I doubt it matters much. Most times they get broken before they burn out.||||reg||1
Z||000011||FieroSTETZ||11-15-1999||10:57 PM||||so are these direct replacements for the whole sealed beam assembly, or just bulbs? i have the original sealed beams, and i DO drive around with brights on cuz they are dim. any reply would be great! oh, how much was it for a pair?||||reg||1
Z||000012||batboy||11-15-1999||11:19 PM||||They are a halogen sealed beam, a direct replacement. I saw them on sale at Advanced Auto Parts the other day for $20 each. I would of bought a pair then, but I needed the $40 to buy an inner tie rod for my 85 GT instead. Maybe next payday.||||reg||1
Z||000013||ggarsk||11-16-1999||05:32 AM||||The K-Mart here has them for 13.00 apiece. Since I'm rebuilding my headlight motors I'm going to put some in while I have everything apart.||||reg||1
Z||000014||Bill Osmer||11-16-1999||11:17 AM||||PEP Boys had a sale on them last weekend for $10 each - it pays to shop around. They're carried at: KMart, AutoZone, PEP Boys, Meijer, Wal-Mart - (all at slightly differnet prices) you get the picture.||||reg||1
Z||000015||Racer 1||11-16-1999||12:19 PM||||Now how much better are these lights then the standard bulbs rating 1-10 10 being the best. I have the fiero store's blue light kit in and dont like them alot. My Big K has HV light also.||||reg||5
Z||000016||theogre||11-17-1999||10:37 AM||||describing the HV lamps........ low beam is like diving into a pool instead of a small stream. big wide flood pattern. You can literaly see around things with them. And this it just on low.<P>((of course you have to make sure any headlight is adjusted right....... but these beat standard halogen no matter how you figure it.))||||reg||1
Z||000017||Racer 1||11-18-1999||02:49 AM||||I just got a set for my GEO Metro. I had the sylvainia&lt;--not right spelling--  ultra vision ones in before I put the HV's in. The HV's low beams was better but was lacking on the high beams. But I dont drive on high beams often so Ill just sick with these in the metro. I might try the Autozone COOL BLUE H4 ones in my fiero.Thanks theogre<BR>||||reg||14
Z||000018||theogre||11-18-1999||10:56 PM||||let us know how those are.......<P>the HV's are probly about the same as manny lamps in Highbeam but then they are a DOT legal street use lamp. <P>Statistcally, and from personal use, most people drive almost the entire life of the lamps in low beam. I've yet to see any low beam lamp compete with these, especially for what these cost. Anyone in an urban area will especially like them.<P>If you have an area where you can use highbeam more often, then yes there are other lamps to try. But do keep in mind that just becuase a chain store sells them they may not be street legal. Even if they are legal, if they seem to bright to the police you make yourself a ticket magnet. This is something the HV bulbs likely won't do to you.<P><BR>No matter what lamps you have, you also have to make sure they are adjusted right. if they aren't you'll get good light in only one "beam" and not the other, or you'll be searching for aircraft and/or goffers in both. True aiming requires an expencive tool, but basic aim can be reliably done on flat ground with any vertical surface or wall. If you want it at best aim without the tool/machine you also need a 50-100 foot tape measure. It helps you make sure you are square to the wall as well as check your aim points.||||reg||1
Z||000019||theogre||11-30-1999||07:30 PM||||After racer's comment above I checked the high beam side of the phillips HV lamps more. I find them to at least as good as any regular halogen, maybe a little better than most.<P>But then i'm pretty carefull about trying to get them aimed right. If yours were off racer then that may be part of you problem. Even a couple degrees off makes a huge diferance, especially in high beam.<P>Headlight aim need to be checked any time the lamps are replaced. each lamp design will aim a little diferant.||||reg||1
Z||000020||GWMORGAN||11-30-1999||09:49 PM||||Ogre......is there a particular way to aim the headlights? Mine's way off. Thanks for info.||||reg||1
Z||000021||theogre||12-01-1999||07:09 PM||||use a flat patch of pavement, flat is important, next to a wall. you also need a stick & pen or tape measure.<P>line the car up square to the wall. You can eye this or measure it out.<P>back the car about 20 feet from the wall.<P>turn on the hight beams. center the light patch of each light vertically with the center of the light. (unplug or block the lamp you aren't working on.) you'r after the brightest part of the light pattern. <P>set the hight of the light patch even with the top of the lamp.<P>back the car away some more and watch how the beams fill in.<P>You do not want the lamps aimed up. you want the high beams flat.||||reg||1
Z||000022||GWMORGAN||12-01-1999||10:57 PM||||Thanks Ogre.||||reg||1
